   "claims": [
    "Check out 78 similar arithmetic calculators ➗ GCF Modulo Average Build your calculator with Builder Add as preferred on Google Our square root calculator estimates the square root of any positive number you want.",
    "Thus, square roots are ubiquitous in planar geometry, while cube roots appear frequently in 3-dimensional geometry.",
    "By contrast, 0 has only one root — itself.",
    "Let's say that you want to estimate the square root of 52: You know that √49 = 7 and √64 = 8, so √52 should be between 7 and 8.",
    "Then, you square 7.3, obtaining 7.3² = 53.29 (as the square root formula says), which is higher than 52.",
    "Let's try and find the square root of 52 again.",
    "You can simplify it to √52 = 2√13 (you will learn how to simplify square roots in the next section) and then substitute √13 ≈ 3.61.",
    "For example, if you enter 17 in the second field, you will find out that 289 is a perfect square.",
    "Suppose that the radicand has a perfect square factor, i.e., 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, and so on (these numbers can be respectively expressed as 2², 3², 4², 5², 6², 7², hence the name).",
    "Then, you can simplify the square root by following these steps: Write down the radicand as a product of a perfect square and another number: in our case, this would be 8 = 4 × 2 because 4 = 2² is a perfect square."
   ],
